e('The disk can only write one block at a time, and power can fail between any two of them.','Storage can execute multiple writes concurrently; it does not ordinarily make an arbitrary multi-block filesystem update atomic. Power can fail with only part persisted.','Deviceconcurrency false.')
e('Appending one 4 KiB block to a file needs three on-disk structures updated:','In a teaching model, appending one newly allocated 4 KiB block updates three structures (real filesystems may update more):','Allocationextra metadata.')
e('`fsck` (file system check) scans the whole disk:','A classic full filesystem checker scans relevant metadata across the filesystem:','Notallrawdatadisk.')
e('Time scales with the size of the file system, not the size of the damage.','Work depends heavily on metadata volume, layout and checking algorithms rather than only the damaged portion.','Notlinearcapacityguarantee.')
e('Recovery time now depends on the size of the journal (tens of MiB up to around 1 GiB on ext4, scaled to the file system), not the size of the disk. That\'s seconds instead of hours.','Ordinary journal replay works over recoverable log transactions rather than performing a full metadata scan. Journal size, pending transactions, device speed and other recovery work determine its duration.\n\n> [!note] Recovery measurement gap\n> No reproducible timing record accompanies this lesson. Fixed seconds-versus-hours guarantees and an unversioned ext4 journal-size range are omitted.','Unsupportedjournalmax/timingremoved.',ext)
e('Modern ext4 can instead add a **checksum** to the commit block, which lets recovery spot an incomplete transaction without that extra wait.','Ext4\'s journal_async_commit mode uses journal checksums to permit a different submission ordering and reject incomplete transactions. Merely enabling checksums is not the same as enabling asynchronous commit, and acknowledged durability still requires the needed writes to persist.','journal_checksum !=async_commit.',ext)
e('Journaling data means **every block is written twice**, once to the journal and once in place. That halves write bandwidth for large writes.','In the simple full-data-journaling model, payload is written to the journal and later home locations. This increases write traffic; achieved throughput is not necessarily halved because batching, layout and device behavior matter.','2xtraffic !=halfbandwidth.',ext)
e('So metadata never points at unwritten blocks: after a crash, a file contains either the new data or the old state, never someone else\'s stale blocks.','This ordering prevents exposing stale contents of newly allocated blocks through a committed metadata update, assuming a correctly functioning storage stack. It does not make arbitrary multi-block application writes atomic or guarantee only a complete old/new application state.','Orderednotapplicationatomic.',ext)
e('So after a power cut you can lose up to about 5 seconds of metadata changes (more for data still in the page cache), but the structure stays consistent.','This controls transaction scheduling, not an application-level maximum loss window; delayed allocation, I/O delays and configuration matter. Use explicit synchronization for acknowledged durability.','Commitperiodnotstrictdurabilitybound.',ext)
e('The **final step** is a single atomic write that switches the root pointer from R to R\'. ZFS calls this root the **uberblock**.','Conceptually, a durable commit selects the new root after its dependencies are stable. Real implementations use checksummed and redundant commit records rather than assuming every root-sector write is magically atomic. ZFS uses uberblocks to identify committed tree state.','Realrootprotocolnotonesectorguarantee.',zf)
e('There\'s no journal and no fsck. The old root makes **snapshots** nearly free: just keep R and don\'t reclaim its blocks.','Copy-on-write reduces reliance on in-place metadata repair, but these systems still have recovery machinery: ZFS uses an intent log for synchronous operations, and Btrfs has a tree log and btrfs check. Snapshots can initially share blocks cheaply; retained old versions consume space as data changes.','ContradictZIL/noBtrfscheckfalse.',zf+['https://btrfs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/btrfs-check.html'])
e('ZFS also stores a **checksum of every block in the pointer that references it**, which makes the tree a Merkle tree. Btrfs checksums every data and metadata block too. A read that doesn\'t match its checksum is detected, and with redundancy (mirrors, RAID-Z) it\'s repaired automatically. That catches silent corruption (bit rot, misdirected writes) that ext4\'s journal can\'t see.','ZFS block-pointer checksums and Btrfs checksums can detect silent corruption within their configured protection scope. Btrfs metadata carries checksums, while data checksumming can be disabled, including for NOCOW data. Recoverable redundancy can let a read find and repair a valid copy; repair is not possible if every copy is bad. This protection is separate from ext4\'s metadata journal, which does not generally checksum ordinary file payload.','Not everyblock/alwaysrepair.',zf+bt)
e('Btrfs lets you disable CoW per file (`chattr +C`).','Btrfs allows NOCOW for newly created/empty files, or inheritance from a directory via chattr +C; existing populated files are not retroactively converted. Shared snapshot extents still require COW on modification.','NOCOWscope.',bt)
e('`write()` copies bytes into the page cache and returns.','Ordinary buffered regular-file write copies data into cache; direct and synchronous I/O have different paths and completion semantics.','Not allwrite buffered.',fs)
e('Returns when they are durable.','Successful completion promises durability under the filesystem/device contract; errors must be checked and faulty hardware can violate it.','Returnfailure not durable.',fs)
e('Faster.','It may avoid work; speed depends on workload and filesystem.','Notalwaysfaster.',fs)
