e('`rename` within one file system is atomic:','This example assumes a trusted stable directory, no concurrent replacement, a previously durable old file, and filesystem/device support for the synchronization contract. It creates a mode-0600 replacement; preserving ownership, ACLs and other metadata needs explicit handling before fsync. If an error occurs after rename, the visible result may already be new even though durability is uncertain.\n\n`rename` within one file system is atomic for namespace visibility:','Stateoldpersist/errorpostrename/permissions.',fs)
e('PostgreSQL, like most software, treated the failure as transient','The affected PostgreSQL behavior treated the failure as transient','Removeunsourcedmostsoftware.',pg)
e('The data was gone, with no error anywhere.','Recovery data could then be lost despite an earlier error having been reported.','ContradictionreportedEIO noerror.',pg)
e('Linux 4.13 and later improved tracking (Jeff Layton\'s `errseq_t` work), so each open file sees a write-back error once. But the error is still reported only once, and the dirty data is still not kept for a retry.','Linux 4.13 introduced improved writeback-error tracking using errseq_t. Relevant open file descriptions can observe newly recorded errors, but this is not an unbounded queue of every failure; sampling/coalescing and filesystem attribution matter. Improved reporting does not guarantee preservation of failed dirty data for a retry.','Not exactone everyfd everyerror.',er+fs)
e('PostgreSQL\'s fix (version 12, back-patched in early 2019 to all supported versions) was to **PANIC on fsync failure**: crash, restart and replay the WAL, which still has the changes. MySQL\'s InnoDB, MongoDB\'s WiredTiger and others made similar changes.','PostgreSQL changed the default to **PANIC on data-file synchronization failure**, documented in release 11.2 (14 February 2019), with data_sync_retry controlling the behavior. Restart and WAL recovery avoid blindly trusting a retry, subject to surviving WAL and storage. Unverified claims about other databases making the same change are omitted.','Versionclarify/unsupportedcrossDBhistory.',pg)
e('Treat a failed `fsync` as "you have lost data in this file since the last successful fsync, and you can\'t get it back from the page cache". Recover from your own log, or fail loudly. Never retry and carry on.','A writeback-related fsync failure makes durability uncertain: data may have been lost, but the error does not prove every byte was lost. A successful retry alone is not recovery. Diagnose the error and use an application recovery/rewrite protocol or fail the operation. Errors such as EINTR have different semantics; do not replace error-specific handling with a claim that every failed fsync proves data loss.','EIOuncertain !=allfail dataloss;EINTR.',fs+pg)
e('Drives that ignore flushes, and RAID controllers with write cache but no battery, can still lose acknowledged writes in a power cut. Enterprise SSDs include **power-loss protection** capacitors for this reason.','A device/controller that dishonors the durability contract can lose writes acknowledged as durable. Volatile caches without batteries can still be safe when correct flush/FUA handling persists data; power-loss protection is another mechanism, and its presence/scope must be checked per product.','No battery !=unsafeflush/allenterprisePLP.',fs)
e('- Copy-on-write file systems (ZFS, Btrfs) never overwrite live blocks and switch roots atomically, which also gives cheap snapshots and end-to-end checksums.','- CoW designs commit new trees while retaining old state; real systems add logs, redundant commit records and configurable data/checksum policies.','Summaryscope.',zf+bt)
e('Treat a failed `fsync` as data loss, never as something to retry (fsyncgate).','Treat writeback-error durability as uncertain; a successful retry alone does not recover possibly lost data.','SummaryEIOvsallfail.',fs+pg)
