f(8,'workedExample','The old committed root references an intact old tree. New blocks not reachable from a valid committed root are not part of that state. Real ZFS commit selection uses redundant validated uberblocks rather than assuming one universally atomic physical write. After selecting a root, intent-log replay can recover separately acknowledged synchronous operations; the question explicitly isolates the tree-commit stage.','Nooneatomic/spaceownershipguarantee.',zf)
f(9,'explanation','This is the risk: some writeback failures can leave no dirty data for the retry to flush. The second success alone does not establish that earlier data persisted.','Maynotalwaysmarkedclean.',pg,o=0)
f(9,'explanation','Correct. Writeback-error handling does not guarantee failed data remains available for retry. Use error-aware recovery; PostgreSQL normally PANICs for data-file sync failure and relies on surviving WAL.','Not exactonceallversions.',pg+er,o=1)
f(9,'explanation','Improved errseq_t tracking is about reporting, not guaranteeing dirty-data retention. Descriptor sampling and error coalescing also matter.','Notreliableeveryerroronce.',er,o=2)
