f(10,'explanation','The episode involved buffered writes and error handling; O_DIRECT was not the required cause. This lesson does not establish that other named engines made identical fixes.','Unsupportedcrossenginehistory.',pg,o=3)
f(10,'workedExample','The historical issue combined lossy writeback-error reporting with failure paths that could discard unwritten data. Linux4.13 improved error tracking using errseq_t, but that does not promise retention of every failed page or a permanent queue of every error. PostgreSQL11.2 documents default PANIC instead of retry, allowing recovery from surviving WAL. The general lesson is possible data loss and uncertain durability after a writeback failure, not that all kernels always discard all data or every fsync error is unrecoverable.','Allversions/no retention universalfalse.',pg+er)
